## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design verifiable, privacy-preserving aggregation systems with end-to-end integrity guarantees using non-interactive zero-knowledge proofs. Candidates should outline the complete architecture, data flows, and anti-tampering mechanisms required for secure distributed computation.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving aggregation with succinct non-interactive arguments of knowledge (SNARKs)
- Tamper-evident ledger chaining for auditability
- Replay protection through nonce management and commitment schemes
- Poisoning resistance in distributed share systems via consistency checks

## Code Example
```javascript
async function verifyProof(proof, publicInputs) {
  // SNARK verification stub
  // Validates that aggregate(sum of shares) == claimed_output
  // without revealing individual share contributions
  return snarklib.verify(verificationKey, publicInputs, proof);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
1. How would you handle device dropout during aggregation rounds?
2. What fallback mechanisms exist if SNARK verification fails?
3. How do you calibrate proof generation time against aggregation freshness requirements?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design consent-aware data pipelines and enforce privacy rules in a concrete API.

## Key Concepts
- Field-level consent
- RBAC and policy enforcement
- Auditability and traceability

## Code Example
```javascript
function applyConsent(userConsent, payload){
  const result={};
  if(userConsent.region) result.region=payload.region;
  if(userConsent.locale) result.locale=payload.locale;
  if(userConsent.model_version) result.model_version=payload.model_version;
  return result;
}
```

